// Names: no new names. The diner's waitress is unnamed and speaks with the Waitress tag (added 2026-10-22, needs a voice) (she/her, as in ch7). Meridian's general
//   counsel, Pellridge's counsel, and the holding company's counsel don't speak and get no pronouns. Alan Mercado is named only.
// Lab (bible ch10 row, "Whose key", not built; whoever builds it should match these): key_inventory.csv holds DB-ENC-01 (settlement
//   database encryption key, AES 256, stored in a configuration file on the database server, custodian pell_dba, created September
//   2019, never rotated), BKP-ENC-01 (backup encryption key, stored on the backup server, custodian Pellridge, never rotated), and
//   SETL-SIGN-2019 (settlement file signing key, stored in SETL-HSM-1); certificate_list.csv holds Meridian Internal CA (one tier,
//   root and issuing on one online virtual server administered by Pellridge, CRL last published June 1, 2021, next update due June
//   8, 2021) and the SETL-SIGN certificate (issued September 14, 2019, expires September 14, 2029, registered with the bank's
//   gateway); custodian_roster.csv: SETL-HSM-1 activation 1 card of 1, holder S. Kade, spare card none, key backup none recorded,
//   ceremony Saturday, September 14, 2019 (S. Kade custodian, one Pellridge engineer operator, witness none, script not attached),
//   activations by card September 14, 2019, September 23, 2023, March 13, 2027, rotation none. Meridian's cryptographic standard
//   (CIO, June 2019): keys stored separately from the data they protect, rotated at least every two years; certificate standard:
//   end entity certificates valid two years at most.
// Facts set here for later chapters: the HSM, once a card activates it, signs whatever the release service sends until the next
//   restart, and it had been active since September 23, 2023 through the event. Pellridge's settlement file archive is object
//   storage in a public cloud provider's US region, in Pellridge's own account, no Meridian users, provider managed keys, deletion
//   at contract end not addressed. DLP: outbound email only, monitor mode since November 2020, rules for card numbers and Social
//   Security numbers only, 22,410 alerts in 2026, none reviewed. Approval app: 30 day sessions, no device passcode check, remote wipe
//   on enrolled company phones only; mobile standard approved by the CIO, 2020. Firewall rule FW-117 (Pellridge office network to
//   the settlement segment, all ports, "cutover, temporary", September 2019, no expiry, never reviewed); Pellridge users sign in to
//   remote access with a password only; the settlement segment holds the release service, the database, SR-N1, and the DOME wall.
//   Ninth floor wireless SETL-FLOOR: WPA2, one shared passphrase unchanged since September 2019, printed on a card at the night desk,
//   onto the settlement segment; guest network separate; rogue access point scan last run 2021. Endpoint report (March 1): 97
//   percent coverage; excluded release servers 1 and 2 (agent not supported on the operating system) and SR-N1 (EX-2019-11); USB
//   storage blocked everywhere except SR-N1. Arthur's file note: clipped to the outside of the 1994 Denver slip's jacket, date
//   stamp October 1994, "Visitor, US regulator, private capacity. Asked about the Denver risk. Nothing further. A.A."; Priyanka
//   read it on the phone at 11:05 AM Friday and sent it to Teresa with the request log at 6 PM London; she didn't open the slip.
//   On every path Henry logs the call and the note with the initials as initials and keeps a private guess (heard path,
//   all[1].picks[7]===2: he signs the entry H.A. under A.A.). Decided: ch11's disclosure names March 26 on every path (one date on
//   the plain path; March 19, March 23, and March 26 on the heard path). Lou closed his end
//   at 5:10 PM Friday: natural causes; C-14 stays on his list as the hotel's problem, "not in her file". 5:12 PM: the ME releases
//   her Monday; she left it all in writing with a lawyer; no service; Henry is on her list, not in her will (decided: graveside,
//   Saturday, April 3 at nine, then the booth; ch12 writes it). The diner tea is the
//   house tea, a hundred bags from the supplier; the waitress says Dot called it the only tea in Denver that didn't pretend to be
//   anything, and sells Henry the box. Henry unpacked the three boxes on Sunday, March 28. Kade, on a call Monday 11 AM with both
//   counsels, proposes rotating the signing key Wednesday, March 31 at 8 AM at the data center (new scheme two of three
//   custodians) and invites Henry to witness; on the best pick Henry declines and Teresa writes by 2 PM asking that the old key's
//   certificate, module log, configuration, and roster be exported and hashed first, with an independent witness and a script,
//   copied to the bureau.
//   Whether the rotation happens is ch12's. Related party papers (Monday 1:40 PM): Pellridge in no related party disclosure 2019 to
//   2026; Kade's conflict declarations "None" every year; onboarding (June 2019) owners "privately held", beneficial owners blank,
//   due diligence "known to management", approved C. Wynn; Pellridge's counsel refuses the share register; counsel for the holding
//   company says it holds no shares in Pellridge, on the Seventeenth Street suite's letterhead. Finding: due diligence failed;
//   related party not established. If ch4 f4s8 wasn't the best pick (all[4].picks[7]!==1), Priyanka asked for the papers through
//   Teresa on March 19. Mercado says yes (Lou, 2:40 PM Monday): W/P 17 examined Tuesday, March 30, 10 AM, police property room,
//   Teresa, Mercado, and Lou present; on the wenttunnelalone path Lou adds that Mercado read his page on the 2:05 line. Nkechi's
//   ch10 tell (fresh shape, unexplained): reading the ceremony record she says "Ours need three of five in the room," then corrects
//   it to "The bureau's." (decided: paid off in ch12). In s6 best she says "A user ID, not a person" (Beth Nyland's ID); Henry: "A key,
//   not a hand." Maisie arrives Monday: Laura's door 7:20 AM, school 7:51, fetched at 3:30; she sleeps in Henry's bed, he
//   has the sofa; she uses the second kitchen chair, price still on. On the ch4 f4s8 pick 0 path Henry writes Giles that the
//   ownership isn't plain, before the finding.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cisa=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa.full||[]).push({
  num: 10, part: `Part Three: The Year`, title: `Keys`, weeks: `Friday, March 26 to Monday, March 29`,
  tasks: [`5A4`,`5A5`,`5A6`,`5A7`,`5A8`,`5A9`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Every settlement file that left Meridian for the Federal Reserve went out signed, and the gateway at the other end trusted the signature. It didn't look at the approvals. Henry saw it at nine on Thursday night, after Lou's car had gone, and asked Teresa Vreeland for everything Meridian kept about its keys, down to who held each one.`,
    `Meridian's counsel sent it at twenty to seven on Friday as an encrypted archive, under a cover letter saying the records were provided without accepting that they mattered. The passphrase didn't come with it. Teresa rang at seven and read it out slowly, then again, and rang off without saying goodbye, which from Teresa was warmth.`,
    `He hung his coat up this time. There were three files inside: the keys, the certificates, and a short roster of people.`,
    `It was seventeen days since the Brown Palace. He'd stopped counting on purpose last week and found he still knew the number, the way you know which stair creaks.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Dot's Workpapers: keys`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Locks`,
        body: [`Encryption is a lock, Lime Street, and I've never yet met a lock that was better than the arrangements for its key. Keep that in your pocket and you'll do fine on this whole subject.`,
          `There are two kinds. Symmetric encryption uses one key to lock and unlock. It's fast, so it does the heavy work, whole databases and whole files, and its weakness is getting the key to the other end without anybody else seeing it. Asymmetric encryption, public key encryption, uses a pair. The public key can go to anyone. The private key stays with one holder. Lock something with my public key and only my private key opens it, so that's how you send me a secret. Real systems use both: the slow pair agrees a one time symmetric key, and the quick key carries the data.`,
          `Then a hash, which isn't encryption at all. It's a fingerprint. Run a file through it and you get a short string; change one comma and the string changes completely, and nobody can work back from the string to the file. Hashes are how you show, a year later in a courtroom, that the evidence you took is the evidence you've got.`,
          `Know where the data is when you protect it. At rest means stored: disks, databases, backups. In transit means crossing a network. Encrypting a laptop's disk protects it when it's switched off and left on a train. It does very little while it's running and signed in, because by then the key is in use.`],
        terms: [[`Symmetric encryption`,`One shared key encrypts and decrypts. Fast; suited to bulk data; the key must reach both ends securely.`],[`Asymmetric (public key) encryption`,`A key pair: anyone may hold the public key, one holder keeps the private key. Encrypt with the recipient's public key for confidentiality.`],[`Hash`,`A fixed length fingerprint of data. Any change to the data changes the hash; it can't be reversed.`],[`Data at rest and in transit`,`Stored data (disks, databases, backups) and data moving across a network, each needing its own protection.`]],
        exam: `Symmetric encryption is fast and suits bulk data; asymmetric encryption solves key distribution. To keep a message confidential, encrypt it with the recipient's public key. A hash detects change. Encryption at rest and in transit protect against different exposures.` },
      { h: `Keys`,
        body: [`A key has a life, and you audit all of it. It's generated properly, from a good source of randomness, not typed in by a tired engineer. It's handed out safely. It's stored apart from what it protects, used for one purpose, and replaced at the end of its cryptoperiod, the length of time the company has decided one key may be trusted. It's revoked the moment anyone thinks it's been exposed. When it's finished, it's destroyed, with a record saying so.`,
          `Stored apart is the one people fail. A key kept in a file on the same server as the database it encrypts is a key left in the lock. It protects you from a burglar who carries the disk out of the building, and from nobody who can sign in. The keys that matter belong in a hardware security module, a tamper resistant box that keeps them inside itself and does the encrypting and signing in there, so the key never comes out in the clear.`,
          `Then the people. Dual control means no one person can use a critical key alone: it takes two, each with their own card or code. Split knowledge means nobody ever holds the whole secret. The usual way is so many cards out of so many, two of three, three of five, held by different people. A company's most important key held by one person is a single point of failure in a good suit.`,
          `Keys get lost, and an encryption key you've lost takes the data with it. So encryption keys are backed up, under the same dual control; that's what backup and escrow are for. Signing keys are another matter. A private signing key generally isn't backed up, because a copy weakens the proof that only its holder signed. The exception is a key the business can't replace quickly, like a certificate authority's, and then any backup stays under the owner's split custody. Either way, a module that only one card in one pocket can wake is one resignation away from a dead key.`,
          `When a key that matters is made, it's made in a ceremony: a written script, named roles, witnesses who aren't the people who'll use it, and a signed record of every step. No script and no witness, and what you've got is an evening with a card in it.`],
        terms: [[`Cryptoperiod`,`The period a key is authorized for use before it must be replaced.`],[`Hardware security module (HSM)`,`A tamper resistant device that stores keys and performs cryptographic operations inside itself.`],[`Dual control`,`A control requiring two or more people to act together, such as two cards to activate a key.`],[`Split knowledge`,`No single person knows or holds a whole key or secret; it's divided among custodians.`],[`Key escrow`,`Holding copies of decryption keys under control so encrypted data can be recovered.`]],
        exam: `Generate keys securely, store them apart from the data (ideally in an HSM), rotate them on a defined cryptoperiod, revoke them when compromised, and destroy them when retired. Critical keys need dual control and split knowledge. Back up encryption keys under the same controls so data can be recovered.` },
      { h: `Who vouches for you`,
        body: [`A digital signature runs the other way round from secrecy. The signer uses their private key, and anyone can check it with the matching public key. If it checks, you know two things: the file was signed with that key, and not one character has changed since. That's integrity and proof of origin. People will tell you it's also non-repudiation, that the signer can't later deny it, and that holds only as far as one person, and nobody else, controls the private key. A signature proves a key. It proves a person only when the key and the person can't be separated.`,
          `It proves nothing about permission. A signed payment is a payment somebody's key signed. Whether anybody approved it is a different control, further up the line, and you test it separately.`,
          `So how do you know a public key really belongs to the company it names, and not to somebody pretending? A certificate. It binds a public key to a name, and it's signed by a certificate authority that everyone relying on it has agreed to trust. A registration authority checks who's asking before the authority issues anything. Each certificate has a validity period, and if its key is exposed before then, the authority revokes it and publishes the fact, in a certificate revocation list or through a service that answers one question at a time, OCSP. A revocation list nobody publishes means nobody can be told to stop trusting.`,
          `The authority's own root key is the crown jewel. It's kept offline, in a module in a locked room, and woken only to sign for the subordinate authorities that do the daily work. If you find a company's root running online, on a server its vendor administers, then the vendor can vouch for anybody in the company's name, and you'll want to know who decided that was all right.`],
        terms: [[`Digital signature`,`A value made with the signer's private key and verified with the public key, showing integrity and the origin key.`],[`Non-repudiation`,`Assurance that a signer can't credibly deny signing, which depends on exclusive control of the private key.`],[`Certificate authority (CA)`,`The trusted party that issues, signs, and revokes certificates binding public keys to identities.`],[`Registration authority (RA)`,`The function that verifies an applicant's identity before the CA issues a certificate.`],[`CRL and OCSP`,`Ways to publish revocation: a signed list of revoked certificates, or a service answering status queries one at a time.`]],
        exam: `A digital signature, made with the private key and verified with the public key, provides integrity, authentication of origin, and non-repudiation, provided the private key is exclusively controlled. It doesn't show authorization. The RA verifies identity; the CA issues and revokes. Keep the root CA offline, and keep revocation status current.` },
      { h: `Walls, and what's inside them`,
        body: [`A network shouldn't be one big room. Segmentation divides it, so the systems that move money sit in a segment of their own with a firewall between them and everything else, and the rules let through only what's needed. A packet filter looks at addresses and ports. A stateful firewall remembers the conversation. An application firewall, or a proxy, reads the traffic itself. Systems the public reaches go in a DMZ, a segment between outside and inside. An intrusion detection system watches traffic and raises the alarm. An intrusion prevention system sits in the path and blocks.`,`Two more you'll meet. A honeypot is a decoy, a system with nothing real on it, set out where an intruder would find it. Nobody has any business touching it, so anything that does is a warning, and it shows you how they work. And zero trust means being inside the network earns you nothing. Every user and device proves who it is, every time, and gets only what that request needs.`,
          `Every firewall rule should have an owner, a reason, and an end date, and somebody should go through the rules every year and take out whatever nobody can explain. The rule labeled temporary is the one I read first.`,
          `A VPN is an encrypted tunnel. Encryption keeps people from reading what goes down it. It says nothing about where the person at the far end can go when they come out. Vendor access needs named accounts, a second factor, rules that reach only the systems the work needs, and an expiry, and it's logged and reviewed like anybody else's.`,
          `Then the endpoints, every laptop, desktop and server. Harden them against a baseline, patch them, run anti-malware or endpoint detection and response, which watches what a machine does and can stop it, and control removable media. Coverage reports always say ninety something percent. Ask for the list of exclusions and look at what's on it.`],
        terms: [[`Network segmentation`,`Dividing a network into separately controlled segments so critical systems are isolated and a compromise is contained.`],[`Firewall`,`A control that permits or blocks traffic between networks by rule: packet filtering, stateful inspection, or application level.`],[`IDS and IPS`,`An intrusion detection system detects and alerts; an intrusion prevention system sits inline and blocks.`],[`Endpoint detection and response (EDR)`,`Software that continuously monitors endpoints for malicious activity and can respond to it.`],[`Hardening`,`Configuring a system to a secure baseline by removing unneeded services and settings.`],[`Honeypot`,`A decoy system with no real data, used to detect and study intruders.`],[`Zero trust`,`A security model that grants nothing for being inside the network and checks every request for identity and need.`]],
        exam: `Segment networks so critical systems sit behind their own controls. Firewall rules need owners, justification, expiry, and periodic review. An IDS detects; an IPS blocks. Third party access needs least privilege, MFA, and logging. Endpoint coverage must include critical systems, and exclusions need risk acceptance. A honeypot detects and studies intruders. Zero trust checks every request, inside the network or out.` },
      { h: `Leaks`,
        body: [`Data loss prevention is software that looks for sensitive data going where it shouldn't. It works in three places: in motion, on the network and in email; at rest, by scanning wherever data is stored; and in use, on the machine itself, where somebody's copying to a stick or printing. It finds what it's told to look for, card numbers and account numbers and documents carrying a label.`,
          `Which is why it's only ever as good as the classification behind it. If the company labeled its most sensitive data as ordinary, the tool will wave it through with a smile. It only sees the channels it's been put on, too. A nightly feed that goes round it might as well be on the moon.`,
          `A new tool runs in monitor mode for a while, raising alerts without blocking anything, so somebody can tune out the false alarms. Then it's switched to block. Monitor mode that never ended, with alerts nobody reads, is a diary.`],
        terms: [[`Data loss prevention (DLP)`,`Tools and rules that detect and stop unauthorized movement or disclosure of sensitive data.`],[`Data in motion, at rest, in use`,`Data crossing networks, stored on media, and being handled on endpoints: the three places DLP works.`],[`Monitor and block modes`,`Alerting only, used while rules are tuned, versus actively stopping matching data.`]],
        exam: `DLP depends first on accurate data classification. It must cover the channels sensitive data actually uses, be tuned, move from monitoring to blocking, and have its alerts reviewed.` },
      { h: `Somebody else's computer`,
        body: [`The cloud is somebody else's computer, rented. In infrastructure as a service you rent the machines and run everything from the operating system up. In platform as a service the provider runs the operating system and you bring the application and the data. In software as a service you rent the whole application and look after your users and your data. Public clouds are shared with strangers, private ones are yours alone, and most companies end up with a hybrid.`,
          `Whatever the model, the provider secures its part and the customer secures the rest: its data, who can get in, and how things are set up. Providers publish this as a shared responsibility model. The accountability for the data never moves, whoever's holding it.`,
          `So read the provider's SOC 2 Type 2 report, and then read the back of it, the complementary user entity controls. Those are the controls the provider assumed you'd run. If nobody runs them, the report is a photograph of a well locked building with your door propped open.`,
          `Then ask the plain questions. Whose account is the data in? Who holds the encryption keys, the provider or the customer? Where is the data kept, and is that allowed? How does it come back, and how is it destroyed when the contract ends? Can you audit it? And with virtual machines, remember that the hypervisor runs all the others, so whoever administers it administers everything on it.`],
        terms: [[`IaaS, PaaS, SaaS`,`Rented infrastructure, a rented platform, or rented software; the customer's share of the work shrinks in that order.`],[`Shared responsibility model`,`The split of security duties between cloud provider and customer, which varies by service model.`],[`Complementary user entity controls`,`Controls a service provider's SOC report assumes the customer operates.`],[`Hypervisor`,`The software that runs virtual machines; its administrators control every machine on it.`]],
        exam: `The customer stays accountable for its data in the cloud. Responsibility shifts with the model: the customer manages most in IaaS and least in SaaS. Review the provider's SOC report and operate the complementary user entity controls. Cover key ownership, location, exit, deletion, and audit rights in the contract.` },
      { h: `Pockets`,
        body: [`Phones go everywhere and get left everywhere. A phone that does company work should be encrypted, locked with a passcode, and enrolled in mobile device management, so the company can enforce those settings and lock or wipe it from a distance. Personal phones are fine if there's a policy, and if the company's part of the phone sits in a managed container that can be wiped without touching the photographs. An app that approves money shouldn't stay signed in for a month.`,
          `Wireless wants current protection, WPA2 at the least and WPA3 where you can. Prefer the enterprise kind, where each person signs in as themselves, over the personal kind, where one passphrase is shared by everyone and outlives every person who's ever left. Keep guests on their own network, and look now and then for access points nobody installed.`,
          `And everything else with an address: cameras, badge readers, the screens on the wall. They come with default passwords and get patched by nobody. Inventory them, change the passwords, and keep them on a segment of their own, away from anything that moves money.`],
        terms: [[`Mobile device management (MDM)`,`Central enrollment and control of mobile devices: enforcing settings, and locking or wiping them remotely.`],[`Containerization`,`Separating company apps and data on a device into a managed, encrypted space that can be wiped on its own.`],[`WPA2 or WPA3 Enterprise`,`Wireless security where each user authenticates individually (802.1X), instead of sharing one passphrase.`],[`Rogue access point`,`A wireless access point connected to the network without authorization.`]],
        exam: `Mobile devices need encryption, passcodes, MDM, and remote wipe; BYOD calls for containerization under a policy. Wireless networks should use individual authentication and be scanned for rogue access points. IoT devices need changed defaults, patching, and segmentation.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f10s1`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A6`, title:`Next to the lock`, q:`Meridian's key inventory lists the database encryption key's storage and custodian. What should Henry conclude?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The key inventory was a single page, which he took as a bad sign. A company that knew its keys usually needed more paper than that.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian key inventory, and cryptographic standard (extracts, via counsel)" say="Three keys on the inventory. The settlement database key, AES 256, is kept in a configuration file on the database server itself, its custodian is pell_dba, and it was made in September 2019 and never rotated; the backup key is on the backup server, Pellridge is its custodian, and it's never been rotated either; and the settlement file signing key, SETL-SIGN-2019, is in the hardware security module, with its custodians on a roster. Under them, the CIO's standard from June 2019: keys are stored apart from the data they protect, and rotated at least every two years.">DB-ENC-01. Settlement database encryption key. Symmetric, AES 256. Stored: configuration file on the database server. Custodian: pell_dba. Created: September 2019. Rotated: never.<br>BKP-ENC-01. Backup encryption key. Stored: backup server. Custodian: Pellridge. Rotated: never.<br>SETL-SIGN-2019. Settlement file signing key. Stored: hardware security module SETL-HSM-1. Custodian: see roster.<br><br>Standard (approved CIO, June 2019): "Keys are stored separately from the data they protect, and rotated at least every two years."</prop>`,`He'd met the custodian's name on Thursday, in the database summary. Four people shared it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pass it, as the database is encrypted at rest.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He notes encryption at rest, in place. It is in place. Its key sits in a file on the same server as the data, readable by the four people who share the account that runs the database, so it keeps the data safe from somebody who walks off with a disk and from nobody else.`,
          why:`Encryption is only as strong as the protection of its key. A key stored with the data it protects, open to that system's administrators, gives little protection against anyone with access to the server.`},
        {t:`Note the key sits with the data, under a shared account.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The standard says keys are kept apart from the data they protect. The key to the settlement database is in a configuration file on the database server, and its custodian is pell_dba, the account four Pellridge administrators share. So the encryption protects a disk that leaves the building. It does nothing about anyone who can sign in to the server, which is the only population that matters here. And it's never been changed, though the same standard says every two years. Two breaches of Meridian's own rule, on one line of one page.`,
          why:`Keys should be stored separately from the data they protect, ideally in dedicated hardware, under named custody, and rotated on a set cryptoperiod. Encryption with the key beside the data protects against theft of media, not against people with access to the system.`},
        {t:`Note the key was made in 2019 and never rotated.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`True, and against the standard. It's also the smaller half. A key changed every two years and kept in the same file as the data would still be a key left in the lock.`,
          why:`Rotation matters, but the first question is where the key lives and who can reach it.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian to move the key into its hardware module.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's probably what Meridian should do. It's also Meridian's decision, and moving a key in the middle of a claim would change the very configuration he's reporting on.`,
          why:`The auditor reports the weakness and may describe what good looks like. Directing the fix is management's job, and a change now would alter the evidence.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s2`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A7`, title:`Ten years`, q:`Henry is reading the certificate list for Meridian's internal CA. What should he conclude about the signing certificate?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The certificate list came next, with a note from counsel that the internal certificate authority was "administered by Pellridge on Meridian's behalf."`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian certificate list (extract)">Issuing authority: Meridian Internal CA. One tier, root and issuing on the same server. Server: virtual, administered by Pellridge, online.<br>Certificate SETL-SIGN. Subject: Meridian Clearing, settlement release. Issued: September 14, 2019. Expires: September 14, 2029. Registered with the bank's gateway: yes.<br>Revocation list: last published June 1, 2021. Next update due: June 8, 2021.<br>Certificate standard: end entity certificates valid two years at most.</prop>`,`The signing certificate was the one the Fed's gateway knew Meridian by. It had been issued on the night of the cutover and was good for another two and a half years.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept the certificate as valid through September 2029.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It is valid until 2029, and that's the trouble with it. If its key were ever in the wrong hands, the list meant to say so hasn't been published since June 2021, and anyone relying on it would go on trusting it for two and a half more years.`,
          why:`A validity period is only half of trust. Revocation has to work, so relying parties can stop trusting a certificate before it expires.`},
        {t:`Flag the ten year term against the two year standard.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The term breaks Meridian's own standard, and it goes in. The authority behind it is where the weakness lives, though: online, run by the vendor, with a revocation list nobody has published since 2021.`,
          why:`Overlong validity is a finding. So are an online root, vendor administration, and stale revocation data, which together mean trust can't be withdrawn.`},
        {t:`Ask Pellridge to publish a fresh revocation list today.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It would have the vendor repair, at the insurer's request, the record under test, and show it exactly where he's looking.`,
          why:`Report the weakness through the agreed channel. Asking the auditee or its vendor to change a record under test alters evidence and signals the direction of the work.`},
        {t:`Note that a revoked certificate would go on being trusted.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The authority that vouches for Meridian's signature runs online, on a server the vendor administers, with its root and its daily signing on one machine. Its revocation list should be republished every week and was last published in June 2021, so if a certificate it issued had to be withdrawn tomorrow, anyone checking would never hear of it. And the signing certificate runs for ten years where Meridian's standard allows two. A certificate is a promise with a date on it. Revocation is how you take the promise back early, and here nobody would hear it taken back.`,
          why:`A CA's root key should be kept offline, with daily issuing done by a subordinate CA, and current revocation information, a revocation list or an online status check, must be published so relying parties can reject revoked certificates. Validity periods should follow policy.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s3`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A6`, title:`One card`, q:`The signing module's roster and ceremony notes are stapled together. What should Henry make of them?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The third file was the roster, two lines long, with the record of the night the key was made stapled behind it.`,`<prop type="doc" title="SETL-HSM-1, settlement signing: custodian roster and ceremony record (extracts)">Activation: 1 card of 1. Card holder: S. Kade. Spare card: none. Key backup: none recorded.<br>Ceremony, Saturday, September 14, 2019: present, S. Kade (custodian) and one Pellridge engineer (operator). Witness: none. Script: not attached.<br>Activations by card: September 14, 2019. September 23, 2023. March 13, 2027. Between activations the key stays live.<br>Rotation: none.</prop>`,(G.tookthekey
        ?`On Thursday he'd carried a badge for twenty minutes that nobody had issued to him, and it was on Lou's list. He knew now how small a gap in custody could be, and how long it stayed on the page.`
        :`Meridian's website said the company ran on trust and speed. He looked at the card holder's name for a while.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Conclude that Kade signed the fraudulent files himself.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`His card woke the module three times since 2019, and the last of those was eight days after the event. The roster shows who held the card. It doesn't put Kade at the release service on any night in March, and once the module is awake it signs whatever it's handed.`,
          why:`Custody of a credential shows who could act, not who did. Link a person to a transaction only with evidence of the transaction.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for the module's activation log before judging.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The log is worth having, and it goes on Teresa's list. But the roster on the desk is a finding as it stands, and it doesn't need the log to be one: one card, one person, no backup, no witness.`,
          why:`More evidence helps, but the custody arrangement itself breaks dual control and needs reporting.`},
        {t:`Record that the key answers to one card and one person.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`One person can wake the key that makes every settlement file Meridian's at the Fed. There's no second card, so no dual control, and no split of the secret, so no split knowledge. There's no spare card and no recovery plan, so if that card were lost, or its holder left, the key would go with it. No one independent watched it made, and there's no script to say how it was done. It's never been rotated, though Meridian's standard says every two years. And once a card has woken the module, it signs whatever the release service hands it until the next restart. It can't tell whether a file had its second approval. None of that says what Simon Kade did. It says the company's most important key answered to one man, and nobody was placed to check him.`,
          why:`High value keys need dual control (two people to act) and split knowledge (no one person holds the whole secret), a witnessed and scripted key ceremony, rotation on a defined cryptoperiod, and a recovery plan under the same controls, such as a spare activation card held by a second custodian.`},
        {t:`Treat the CEO's own custody as a strong control.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It feels like seriousness at the top. It's the opposite of what key custody is for: the person with the most authority in the company holds, alone, the one control that was meant to need two people.`,
          why:`Seniority isn't a key control. Concentrating custody in one person, however senior, removes dual control and accountability.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A5`, title:`Monitor only`, q:`Pellridge's 2026 security report has a page on data loss prevention. What should Henry take from it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At half past ten he went looking for whatever at Meridian would have noticed settlement data leaving. Pellridge's annual security report had a page on it.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Data loss prevention (Pellridge security report to Meridian, 2026, extract)">Coverage: outbound email. File transfers, the settlement archive feed, endpoints: not covered.<br>Mode: monitor (alert only) since it went live in November 2020.<br>Rules: card numbers; Social Security numbers (RESTRICTED). Merchant bank details: classified INTERNAL, no rule.<br>Alerts, 2026: 22,410. Alerts reviewed: 0.</prop>`,`He did the sum on the back of the roster. About sixty a day, every day of last year, read by nobody.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Note it watches only email and blocks nothing.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Meridian's data loss prevention watches outbound email and nothing else, so the nightly feed of every settlement file to Pellridge's archive goes straight past it. It has run in monitor mode since the day it was switched on, which is right for a month of tuning and wrong for six years. Its rules come from the classification register, where merchant bank details are INTERNAL, so the data most worth taking never matches anything at all. Twenty two thousand alerts last year, and not one of them read.`,
          why:`DLP must cover the channels sensitive data actually uses, move from monitoring to blocking once tuned, have its alerts reviewed, and rest on accurate classification. Data that's misclassified is invisible to it.`},
        {t:`Accept it as the DLP control the policy requires.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Meridian has a product with DLP on the box. It watches email, warns no one, and has no rule for the data Meridian moves most, because the label on that data says it doesn't need one.`,
          why:`A control's existence isn't its effectiveness. Check scope, mode, rules, and whether anyone acts on the output.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian to switch it to block mode today.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The insurer would be setting the insured's security controls in the middle of a claim. Untuned, it would also block the payroll and half the claim correspondence by lunchtime.`,
          why:`Changing controls is management's decision. The auditor reports the weakness, and an untuned switch to blocking does harm of its own.`},
        {t:`Sample last year's alerts, and see what they caught.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Twenty five alerts, nearly all of them staff emailing their own card statements home. It's a fair test of what the tool sees. The finding is what it doesn't see: the archive feed, and every merchant's bank details.`,
          why:`Testing output is useful, but establish coverage and rules first. What a DLP tool can't see matters more than what it flagged.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s5`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A8`, title:`Pellridge's account`, q:`Pellridge's hosting summary covers the settlement archive in the public cloud. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const heard=((all&&all[1]&&all[1].picks)||{})[7]===2,hid=!!G.hidconflict,lg=!!G.loggedin;
        return [`Priyanka rang from Lime Street at five past eleven, five past five in London, late for her on a Friday.${hid?` Since the fifteenth she'd read every page he wrote before Giles did.`:``}`,`<span class="said" data-who="Priyanka">"Teresa asked us on Wednesday for anything Vellacott holds that touches Ms. Kessler. The police lawyer will want to know. All the archive had was the request log, so I went to the jacket it's logged against."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"And the slip?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Priyanka">"I didn't open it. You said not yet. There's a file note clipped to the front of the jacket. I'll read it to you."</span> He heard a drawer open in London.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Underwriter's file note, clipped to the jacket of a 1994 Denver slip (read by Priyanka)"><span class="said" data-who="Priyanka">Visitor, US regulator, private capacity. Asked about the Denver risk. Nothing further. A.A.<br>Date stamp: October 1994.</span></prop>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Priyanka">"It goes to Teresa tonight with the log. I wanted you to hear it from me first."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Priyanka">"I don't know whose initials they are. The slip would say."</span>`,
        (heard
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Thank you,"</span> he said, and nothing else. Priyanka waited, the way people do when they're leaving you room, and then said she'd send it at six. He logged the call with the time and the words of the note, the initials as initials, and signed the entry the way he signed everything. H.A., under A.A. One letter apart. What he thought, he kept.`
          :`There had been a great many underwriters in the Room in those years, and two letters weren't a name. He thought of one anyway. His father had asked on the first Tuesday whether Dorothy had found him, which was a sentence on a care home phone and evidence of nothing in London. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Send it to Teresa. Say the slip hasn't been opened."</span> He logged the call with the time and the words of the note. Then he sat for a minute with his hand flat on the desk.`),
        `The next file was about the archive that had got Meridian back on its feet in March, and whose computer it lived on.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Pellridge settlement file archive (Pellridge hosting summary, via counsel, extract)">Location: object storage, public cloud provider, US region. Account holder: Pellridge. Meridian users with access: none.<br>Encryption: provider managed keys, in Pellridge's account.<br>Retention: 7 years. Return or deletion at contract end: not addressed.<br>Provider's SOC 2 Type 2 report: available (scope: the provider's infrastructure).</prop>`,
        ...(lg?[`He'd been inside one of Meridian's systems this week, once, at 12:41 in the morning. Once was going to cost him a morning in court.`]:[])]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Rely on the provider's SOC 2 report to cover the archive.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The provider's report covers the provider: its buildings, its hardware, its staff. It says nothing about who Pellridge lets into its own account, or what Meridian ever asked for. Those halves were always the customer's.`,
          why:`Under the shared responsibility model the provider secures its infrastructure and the customer stays responsible for data, access, and configuration. A provider's SOC report covers only its part, and lists complementary controls the customer must run.`},
        {t:`Ask Pellridge for his own read access to the cloud archive.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It puts an insurer's investigator inside the vendor's cloud account browsing evidence, outside the agreed channel, with his own session written into the logs he'd be testing.`,
          why:`Obtain evidence as preserved exports through the agreed channel. Direct access to a vendor's systems compromises independence and the evidence.`},
        {t:`Note Meridian holds neither the account nor the keys.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Every settlement file Meridian has sent since 2019 is stored in a cloud account that belongs to its vendor. Meridian has no user in it and no copy of its logs. The data is encrypted, by keys the provider manages inside Pellridge's account, so the only people who can unlock Meridian's records are Pellridge and the provider. The contract says nothing about where the data must stay, how Meridian gets it back, or what happens to it when the contract ends. The provider has done its half. Meridian never did its own, and couldn't now without asking Pellridge's leave. In March it got its business back from that archive on Pellridge's terms, and it didn't know they were the only terms there were.`,
          why:`A cloud customer stays accountable for its data. It should hold or control the account, the encryption keys (customer managed where the risk warrants), access, and logs, with contract terms on location, retrieval, and deletion. Outsourcing the account outsources the control, not the accountability.`},
        {t:`Get the provider's SOC 2 report and read its scope first.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It'll come, and the scope line will say what he expects: the provider's infrastructure. The complementary controls at the back are the customer's to run, and the customer of record here is Pellridge.`,
          why:`Reading the provider's report and its complementary user entity controls is right. The finding is that Meridian, which owns the data, holds none of those controls.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s6`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A7`, title:`Whose hand`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Teresa had sent the bureau the custody records at two. At twenty to four the street door went, and then the stairs. Agent Obi took her coat off and sat in the client's chair, and put the ceremony record on the desk between them.`,`She read it from the bottom up. Witness: none. Script: not attached.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Ours need three of five in the room."</span> She stopped, and then added, as if she were fixing a typo, <span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"The bureau's."</span>`,`He hadn't asked whose.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"So every file that went to the Fed the week of the fifth carried Meridian's signature, made by a key that answers to one man's card."</span> She looked up. <span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"Is that his signature?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say it's his, as a digital signature binds its owner.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`That's what non-repudiation promises, and it promises it only when one person controls the private key and nobody else can use it. Here a machine holds the key and uses it for whatever the release service sends.`,
          why:`Non-repudiation depends on exclusive control of the private key. A signature made by a shared signing service proves the service signed, not who caused it.`},
        {t:`Say it proves the key and the file, not a person.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"It's the key's."</span> He gave her the reasons. A digital signature is made with a private key and checked with the public one in the certificate. When it checks, two things are true: the file was signed by that key, and it hasn't changed by a character since. That's worth a great deal, and it isn't a person. The key lives in a module that, once a card has woken it, signs whatever the release service hands it, and it had been awake since September 2023. It doesn't know about approvals. It doesn't know who sat at SR-N1. <span class="said" data-who="Nkechi">"A user ID, not a person,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"A key, not a hand."</span> She writes that down, which he's never seen her do.`,
          why:`A digital signature verified with the public key shows integrity and the origin key. It identifies a person only as far as one person exclusively controls that key, and it says nothing about whether a transaction was authorized.`},
        {t:`Say it shows the files came from Meridian's system.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`True as far as it goes. It leaves out the half she'll need in court: that the signature also proves the files weren't changed after signing, and that it can't name anyone.`,
          why:`A signature shows origin and integrity. Explaining its limits matters as much as its strengths.`},
        {t:`Tell her it means each file had its second approval.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The module never asks. It signs what it's given. A signed file can be a file nobody approved at all.`,
          why:`Signing isn't authorization. Approval controls sit before the signature and are tested separately.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s7`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A9`, title:`In their pockets`, q:`Meridian's settlement approvals run on a mobile app. What should Henry take from its enrollment report?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const lp=((all&&all[4]&&all[4].picks)||{})[6],yes4=lp===3||lp===0;
        return [`Lou's text had come at ten past five on Friday, while Henry was locking the office.`,`<prop type="text" from="Lou" time="5:10 PM">Closed my end. Natural causes. C-14 stays on my list as the hotel's problem. It's not in her file.</prop>`,`<prop type="text" from="Lou" time="5:12 PM">ME releases her Monday. She left it all in writing with a lawyer. No service. You're on her list. Not the will. The list.</prop>`,`He'd read it on the landing and stood there until the light timed out.`,`At nine on Saturday he took the booth by the window and sat on his own side of it. The waitress brought coffee without asking, and didn't ask whether anyone was coming. When he asked what the tea was, she went and got the box.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Waitress">"House tea. Hundred bags from the supplier, cheapest they do. She knew. She said it was the only tea in Denver that didn't pretend to be anything."</span> She sold him the box at cost and wouldn't take a tip on it.${yes4?``:` On the way back he bought a toaster, the second one the shop had, because the first was the color of a fire engine.`}`,`At the office he opened the last of Friday's batch, which was about where Meridian's approvers did their approving.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Settlement approval app (Meridian mobile standard and enrollment report, extracts)">Approvers: 14. On company phones enrolled in device management: 5. On personal phones, not enrolled: 9.<br>App sign in: stays signed in for 30 days. Device passcode: not checked. Remote wipe: enrolled company phones only.<br>Standard: "Personal devices may be used for approvals." Approved: CIO, 2020.</prop>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept the app's own sign in as a strong enough control.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The sign in is the approver's own account, and it stays signed in for a month on an unmanaged phone, with no passcode check. Whoever holds the phone holds the approval.`,
          why:`Authentication that persists on unmanaged devices shrinks to possession of the device. Mobile access to critical functions needs device controls.`},
        {t:`Flag the thirty day stay signed in as the weak point.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The session length goes in, and it's bad. But the session lives on the phones, and the phones are the half nobody manages.`,
          why:`Session limits matter. The larger weakness is approval from unmanaged devices with no passcode or wipe control.`},
        {t:`Conclude a lost or stolen phone explains March.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Nothing in the file says any phone was lost, or that any March approval came from one. A weakness shows what was possible, and that's all it shows.`,
          why:`A control weakness shows what could have happened. Linking it to an event needs evidence of the event.`},
        {t:`Note nine of fourteen approve on phones nobody manages.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The second approval on every file over ten thousand dollars, the control Dot called the capstone, can be given from a personal phone Meridian has never enrolled and can't wipe, through an app that stays signed in for thirty days and doesn't care whether the phone has a passcode. Nine of the fourteen approvers do exactly that. Leave one of those phones in a taxi and the approval goes with it until somebody notices. Personal phones can be fine for this, with the company's part fenced off in a managed container that can be locked and wiped on its own. Meridian approved the phones and skipped the fence.`,
          why:`Mobile devices used for critical transactions should be managed: enrollment, enforced passcodes and encryption, session limits, and remote lock and wipe. For personal devices, containerization separates and protects company data under a BYOD policy.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s8`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A4`, title:`Every port`, q:`The firewall export shows rule FW-117, created for the 2019 cutover. How should Henry read it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The network papers took the afternoon. Most of them were diagrams, and the diagrams were from 2019. He went past them to the firewall export, which was current.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Settlement network: firewall rules and vendor access (extract, via counsel)">Rule FW-117. From: Pellridge office network. To: settlement segment. Ports: all. Action: allow. Reason: "cutover, temporary." Created: September 2019. Expiry: none. Last reviewed: never.<br>Vendor connection: site to site tunnel, encrypted. Pellridge staff sign in to remote access: password only.<br>Settlement segment holds: release service, settlement database, SR-N1, the DOME wall.</prop>`,`Temporary, in September 2019. He'd heard that word all fortnight.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Note it opens every port to the vendor, with no expiry.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`One rule, written for a weekend in 2019 and never taken out, lets anything on Pellridge's office network reach anything on Meridian's settlement segment, on any port. It has no owner, and no one has looked at it since. The people coming through it sign in with a password and nothing more. And the segment it opens onto holds the release service and the database beside the night desk and the wall, in one room with no walls inside it. Vendor access should reach only the systems the work needs, through named accounts with a second factor, under rules that expire. This one was temporary for seven years.`,
          why:`Third party network access should follow least privilege: specific rules with owners, expiry, and periodic review, multi factor authentication, and segmentation so critical systems sit behind their own controls.`},
        {t:`Pass it, as the tunnel is encrypted end to end.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Encryption keeps strangers from reading what goes down the tunnel. It does nothing about where the people at the other end can go once they're through, and here that's everywhere.`,
          why:`Encryption protects confidentiality in transit. It isn't access control. Vendor connections need least privilege rules, strong authentication, and review.`},
        {t:`Record the password only sign in as the finding.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The missing second factor goes in. The rule that lets those passwords reach every port next to the money is the larger finding.`,
          why:`MFA on vendor access is essential. Overbroad, unreviewed firewall rules are the larger weakness.`},
        {t:`Scan the segment himself, and see what the rule opens.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It would be testing the insured's network without authorization, in the middle of a claim and a federal case. Whatever it found would be evidence he'd made himself.`,
          why:`Technical testing needs explicit authorization and an agreed scope. Unauthorized scanning is improper and may be unlawful.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s9`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A6`, title:`Two channels`, q:`Priyanka will review the encrypted key records first thing London time. How should Henry get them to her?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const lp=((all&&all[4]&&all[4].picks)||{})[6],yes4=lp===3||lp===0,hid=!!G.hidconflict;
        return [`On Sunday the clocks went forward in London, and his father's watch, still seven hours behind, was right again.`,`He spent the morning on the three boxes in the kitchen. Two held kitchen things he'd bought in April and never used. The third was books, and he put them on the shelf in the order they came out. ${yes4?`The toaster came out of its own box last. He plugged it in and made a slice of toast he didn't want, to see.`:`The new toaster went on the counter, and he made a slice of toast he didn't want, to see.`} He put clean sheets on his bed for Maisie, found a pillow for the sofa, and stood the box of tea on the shelf where she'd see it.`,`At noon an email came from Priyanka. ${hid?`She'd review the key records first thing in the morning, London time, before he was up. That was how it went now.`:`Giles wanted a second reader on anything with a chairman's name in it, and she'd do the key records first thing in the morning, London time, before he was up.`}`,`The records were still in the encrypted archive Meridian's counsel had sent on Friday. The passphrase had come to Teresa by phone, and from Teresa to him the same way.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Email her the archive, then the passphrase separately.`, s:0, flag:`emailedkey`, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`Two emails, a minute apart, to the same inbox. Anyone who can read one can read the other, and the archive and the words that open it now sit side by side on Vellacott's mail servers and in every backup of them.`,
          why:`Sending encrypted data and its key or passphrase through the same channel defeats the encryption. Keys travel separately, out of band.`},
        {t:`Unzip it and send the files plain inside Vellacott.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`Inside Vellacott is a mail system, its backups, a laptop on the Tube, and whoever's reading over her shoulder there. The encryption was the one thing about these files that was working.`,
          why:`Sensitive evidence stays encrypted in transit and at rest, internal recipients included. Removing protection for convenience exposes it.`},
        {t:`Upload the archive, then ring her with the passphrase.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The archive goes up to Vellacott's secure transfer site at 6:20, addressed to Priyanka alone. The passphrase waits. At ten past two in the morning his alarm goes, ten past nine in London, and he rings her desk and reads it out once, and she reads it back. The lock goes one way and the key goes another, so a slip in one channel doesn't hand anybody both. He's asleep again by twenty past.`,
          why:`Encrypted data and its key should travel by separate channels, so compromising one doesn't expose both. Use the organization's secure transfer for the data and an out of band channel, such as a call, for the key.`},
        {t:`Upload the archive, then text her the passphrase.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Two channels, which is the point. A text sits on her phone and his until somebody deletes it, readable by anyone who picks up either one.`,
          why:`Separating the key from the data is right. A message that persists is a weaker channel than a call that leaves nothing behind.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s10`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A9`, title:`One password`, q:`The 2021 wireless survey covers the ninth floor's settlement network. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Laura opened the door at twenty past seven in socks, with her reading glasses pushed up into her hair, and Maisie came out past her with a bag that looked packed for a month.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Laura">"She'll tell you she can walk from the corner,"</span> Laura said. <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"She can't. The door, please."</span> Then, <span class="said" data-who="Laura">"Thank you, Henry."</span>`,`He had her at the school door at seven fifty one. She got out without saying goodbye, then came back and knocked on the window and said it, and went in.`,`By half past nine he was at the desk on Wazee with the last of the network papers, a wireless survey Pellridge had done for Meridian in 2021 and never repeated.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Ninth floor wireless (Pellridge survey for Meridian, 2021, extract)">Network SETL-FLOOR. WPA2, one shared passphrase. Connects to: settlement segment. Passphrase last changed: September 2019. Known to: settlement staff, Pellridge night operations. Printed on a card at the night desk.<br>Guest network: separate, internet only.<br>Scan for unauthorized access points: last run 2021.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask the night lead to change the passphrase tonight.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The vendor's engineer would be taking orders from the insurer, outside the agreed channel, and changing the network under review before anyone had kept a record of how it was.`,
          why:`Report weaknesses; don't direct changes, least of all to systems under examination.`},
        {t:`Record one shared passphrase, unchanged from 2019.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The ninth floor's wireless uses one passphrase for everybody, opens straight onto the settlement segment, and hasn't changed since the cutover, through every leaver and every Pellridge rotation. It's printed on a card at the night desk. A floor like that should use the enterprise kind of wireless, where each person signs in as themselves and leaving the company ends their access that day, and somebody should look for access points the company never installed more often than once since 2021. It's one more door that opens for a group, and this one doesn't even keep a list.`,
          why:`Wireless access to sensitive networks should use individual authentication, the enterprise mode of WPA2 or WPA3, connect through controlled segments, change shared secrets when people leave, and be scanned regularly for rogue access points.`},
        {t:`Pass it, as WPA2 is a recognized encryption standard.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`WPA2 keeps the air from being read from the street. Everybody who's worked a night on the ninth floor since 2019 has the passphrase, and the network can't tell one of them from another, or a leaver from somebody still employed.`,
          why:`Wireless security depends on how users authenticate as well as on encryption. A shared passphrase that never changes gives no individual accountability and outlives every leaver.`},
        {t:`Report the rogue access point scan last run in 2021.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`A stale scan goes in, rightly. The passphrase on the card is the live weakness.`,
          why:`Rogue access point scanning matters, but a shared, unchanged credential into the settlement network is the larger weakness.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s11`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A7`, title:`A witness`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const told=!!G.toldkade;
        return [`At eleven Teresa put a call through with Meridian's general counsel on the line and, after a short delay, Simon Kade.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kade">"Mr. Ashby. I'm told I'm the only key in the building. I'd have said I was the safest place for it."</span> He sounded as if he were smiling. <span class="said" data-who="Kade">"We had sixteen people in IT when we built the platform, and I didn't know half of them. I knew me."</span>`,
        ...(told?[`<span class="said" data-who="Kade">"You told me about the contract on the fifteenth and we'd fixed it by quarter past four. Let me fix this one where you can watch."</span>`]:[]),
        `He'd added it to the board's remediation plan on Friday, he said, by email to the directors: a new signing key, under a new scheme that needed two custodians out of three. He wanted to do it on Wednesday at eight, at the data center.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kade">"Come and watch. Nobody will ever be able to say again that it was done without a witness."</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Accept the invitation and witness Wednesday's ceremony.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`A witness is what the old key never had, and the witness shouldn't be him. His name would go on the record of a control he's meant to be reporting on, as the man who watched it done properly, while the old key's record is still open in his file.`,
          why:`An auditor may observe a key ceremony to gather evidence, but signing its record as the ceremony's witness makes the auditor part of the control. Here Henry is the insurer's investigator, the old key's record is still under examination, and the invitation comes from the key's only custodian, so taking the role would impair his independence.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian to wait on the new key until his report.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Kade agrees with a grace that costs him nothing. Meridian keeps a key it shouldn't for another fortnight because the insurer asked, and the record of the old one is no safer for the wait.`,
          why:`Fixing a weakness is management's call, and an auditor shouldn't hold it up. What the auditor needs is the evidence preserved before the change, not the change postponed.`},
        {t:`Ask Kade to hand the card to the board chair today.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's an instruction from the insurer's investigator to the insured's chief executive about a control the board owns. Kade would do it, graciously, and the record would show who told him to.`,
          why:`Directing management's control changes impairs independence. Report the custody weakness; the board decides the remedy.`},
        {t:`Decline and have counsel ask to keep the old key's records.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"That's generous. I'll say no, I'm afraid, and Teresa will put the reasons in writing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Teresa">"By two, copied to the bureau,"</span> Teresa says. Replacing the key is right, and it's Meridian's to do. Before the old one is retired, its certificate, the module's log, its configuration and the roster are to be exported and hashed, with the originals kept. The witness should be independent of Meridian and of the insurer, the ceremony should follow a written script, and everyone present should sign the record. The new key should have its own backup under the same two of three. Kade says he couldn't agree more. He sounds as if he means it, which Henry has learned isn't evidence of anything.`,
          why:`Key rotation is a management action. Before records change, ask through counsel that the old key's records be preserved with integrity controls, and expect a scripted ceremony with independent witnesses and dual control. Don't become part of the control yourself.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s12`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5A4`, title:`Exclusions`, q:`Pellridge's endpoint report shows 97 percent coverage. What should Henry conclude?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The last paper of the morning was Pellridge's endpoint report, which had a big number at the top. He started at the bottom.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Endpoint protection coverage, March 1 (Pellridge, extract)">Coverage: 97 percent of managed endpoints.<br>Excluded: release service servers 1 and 2 (agent not supported on the operating system). SR-N1 (exception EX-2019-11, "monitoring desk").<br>USB storage: blocked on all endpoints except SR-N1.</prop>`,`Two servers and a desk. Of everything Meridian owned, they sat nearest the money.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Note the exclusions, the machines nearest the money.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Ninety seven percent is a number about laptops. The exclusions are the point. The two servers that send Meridian's money to the Fed run with no endpoint protection, because their operating system is too old for the agent, the same unsupported system on the sheet from Tuesday night with a second cost attached. SR-N1, the desk nobody owns, is excluded under the exception that switched off its screen lock, and it's the one machine in the company where a USB stick still works. Nothing watches what happens on any of the three. Exclusions need an owner, a reason, something in their place, and an end date. These have a reason each, and nothing else.`,
          why:`Endpoint controls (anti-malware or EDR, hardening, removable media control) must cover critical systems. Exclusions need risk acceptance, compensating controls, and expiry, and unsupported platforms that can't run security agents compound the risk.`},
        {t:`Take 97 percent as strong coverage by any standard.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's high coverage of the machines that matter least. The three exclusions are the release service and the night desk, and a percentage across every laptop in the company hides them completely.`,
          why:`Coverage figures can mask critical gaps. Check what's excluded and why, starting with the systems that carry the most risk.`},
        {t:`Conclude the settlement data left SR-N1 on a USB stick.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`A port that works is a port that works. Nothing in any record puts a stick in it.`,
          why:`A weakness shows what was possible. It isn't evidence of what happened.`},
        {t:`Write up SR-N1, its USB port and its exception.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The desk goes in, port and exception both, and it should. The two servers that sign and send every file run with nothing watching them at all, and they're the bigger gap.`,
          why:`Removable media is a real risk. Unprotected critical servers are a larger one, and both belong in the finding.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f10s3`, title:`Old keys`,
     setup:[`Meridian's settlement signing key was generated on Saturday, September 14, 2019, and has never been rotated. Meridian's cryptographic standard says keys are rotated at least every two years. The signing certificate runs from September 14, 2019 to September 14, 2029, and Meridian's certificate standard allows two years at most. Count to Friday, March 26, 2027.`],
     fields:[{label:`Days since the key was generated`,answer:2750,tol:0},{label:`Rotations due and missed by March 26, 2027`,answer:3,tol:0},{label:`Days since the first rotation was due`,answer:2019,tol:0},{label:`Certificate term as a multiple of the allowed maximum`,answer:5,tol:0}],
     solution:[`September 14, 2019 to September 14, 2026 is seven years: 7 × 365 = 2,555, plus the leap days of February 2020 and February 2024, so 2,557. Then to March 26, 2027: 16 (to September 30) + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 28 + 26 = 193. Total 2,750 days on one key.`,`Rotations were due on September 14 of 2021, 2023, and 2025. The next falls in September 2027. So 3 missed.`,`September 14, 2021 to September 14, 2026 is five years: 5 × 365 = 1,825, plus February 29, 2024, so 1,826. Add the same 193 days: 2,019 days overdue on the first one alone.`,`Ten years against a two year limit: 10 ÷ 2 = 5 times the term the standard allows.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f10s6`, title:`Whose key?`, intro:`Dot kept a page headed "Lock it, or sign it?" A sender and a recipient each have a key pair. For each job or question, pick the key.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Encrypt a file so only the recipient can read it.`, choices:[`Sender's private key`,`Sender's public key`,`Recipient's public key`,`Recipient's private key`], answer:2, why:`Anyone can lock with the recipient's public key. Only the recipient's private key unlocks it.`},
      {prompt:`Decrypt that file when it arrives.`, choices:[`Sender's private key`,`Sender's public key`,`Recipient's public key`,`Recipient's private key`], answer:3, why:`The recipient's private key, which only the recipient holds.`},
      {prompt:`Sign a settlement file so the bank knows it came from the sender.`, choices:[`Sender's private key`,`Sender's public key`,`Recipient's public key`,`Recipient's private key`], answer:0, why:`A signature is made with the signer's private key.`},
      {prompt:`Check the signature on a file that claims to come from the sender.`, choices:[`Sender's private key`,`Sender's public key`,`Recipient's public key`,`Recipient's private key`], answer:1, why:`Anyone can verify with the signer's public key, usually taken from the certificate.`},
      {prompt:`A sender denies sending a signed file. Whose key's custody decides how much the signature proves?`, choices:[`Sender's private key`,`Sender's public key`,`Recipient's public key`,`Recipient's private key`], answer:0, why:`Non-repudiation rests on exclusive control of the signer's private key. If others could use it, the signature proves the key, not the person.`},
      {prompt:`Wrap a one time session key so only the bank can unwrap it.`, choices:[`Sender's private key`,`Sender's public key`,`Recipient's public key`,`Recipient's private key`], answer:2, why:`The session key is a secret for the recipient, so it's locked with the recipient's public key. The fast symmetric session key then carries the data.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Where should the key that encrypts a production database BEST be kept?`, opts:[`On the same server as the data`,`In an HSM, apart from the data`,`In the application's source code`,`In a folder all the DBAs share`], a:1, why:`A key stored with the data it protects gives little protection. Dedicated hardware, separate from the data and under named custody, protects the key even from people with access to the server.`, task:`5A6`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A signing key can only be used when two custodians each present a card. Which principle does this BEST illustrate?`, opts:[`Least privilege`,`Need to know`,`Defense in depth`,`Dual control`], a:3, why:`Dual control requires two or more people to act together, so no single person can use the key alone.`, task:`5A6`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A sender wants a file that only the recipient can read. Which key should encrypt it?`, opts:[`The recipient's public key`,`The sender's public key`,`The sender's private key`,`The recipient's private key`], a:0, why:`Anything encrypted with the recipient's public key can be decrypted only with the recipient's private key.`, task:`5A6`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`Full disk encryption on a laptop BEST protects against which risk?`, opts:[`Malware while the user is signed in`,`Phishing emails sent to the user`,`Data exposure if it's lost while off`,`A weak password on the user's account`], a:2, why:`Disk encryption protects data at rest. Once the machine is running and signed in, the key is in use and the data is readable to anyone using it.`, task:`5A6`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`What does a verified digital signature PRIMARILY provide?`, opts:[`Confidentiality of the message`,`Integrity and proof of origin`,`Proof the payment was approved`,`Faster transmission of the file`], a:1, why:`A signature made with the private key and verified with the public key shows the content is unchanged and which key signed it. It doesn't encrypt the message or show authorization.`, task:`5A7`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A company's certificate revocation list hasn't been published for years. What is the GREATEST risk?`, opts:[`Certificates may expire early`,`No new certificates can be issued`,`Encryption may run more slowly`,`Revoked ones may still be trusted`], a:3, why:`Relying parties check revocation to stop trusting compromised certificates. A stale list means revoked certificates can go on being accepted until they expire.`, task:`5A7`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`Which practice BEST protects a certificate authority's root key?`, opts:[`Keeping it offline in an HSM`,`Renewing it every single month`,`Storing it on the issuing server`,`Sharing it with the vendor`], a:0, why:`Root keys are kept offline in secure hardware and used only to sign subordinate CAs, which do the daily issuing.`, task:`5A7`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`In a PKI, which function verifies an applicant's identity before a certificate is issued?`, opts:[`The certificate revocation list`,`The relying party`,`The registration authority`,`The hardware security module`], a:2, why:`The registration authority identifies and authenticates applicants; the certificate authority then issues and signs the certificate.`, task:`5A7`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`Staff on a floor connected to the payment network share one WPA2 passphrase that hasn't changed in years. Which control BEST fixes this?`, opts:[`A longer, stronger shared passphrase`,`Hiding the network's name`,`Individual sign in (802.1X)`,`Changing it every January`], a:2, why:`WPA2 or WPA3 Enterprise signs each user in with their own credentials through 802.1X, so access is individual, logged, and ends when a person leaves. A longer or more often changed passphrase is still shared, and hiding the network's name doesn't stop anyone who knows it.`, task:`5A9`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A vendor's encrypted tunnel reaches every system on the settlement network. What is the auditor's MAIN concern?`, opts:[`The tunnel's encryption strength`,`Access beyond what's needed`,`The cost of the vendor's line`,`The speed of the connection`], a:1, why:`Encryption protects data in transit, not where users can go. Vendor access should be limited to the systems the work needs, with strong authentication and review.`, task:`5A4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`What is the PRIMARY security benefit of network segmentation?`, opts:[`It speeds up backups`,`It saves IP addresses`,`It simplifies the firewall`,`It contains a compromise`], a:3, why:`Separating critical systems into their own segments, with controlled traffic between them, limits how far an intrusion or malware can spread.`, task:`5A4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`Which device detects an attack in progress AND can block it?`, opts:[`An intrusion prevention system`,`An intrusion detection system`,`A central log server`,`A network packet sniffer`], a:0, why:`An IPS sits inline and can block traffic. An IDS detects and alerts but doesn't block.`, task:`5A4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`An endpoint protection report shows 97 percent coverage. What should the auditor examine FIRST?`, opts:[`The vendor's license terms`,`Which devices are excluded`,`How fast the agent updates`,`Staff training on the agent`], a:1, why:`A high percentage can hide critical gaps. The exclusions, and whether they include critical systems, matter most.`, task:`5A4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A DLP tool's effectiveness depends MOST on which of these?`, opts:[`The number of alerts it raises`,`The speed of the mail gateway`,`How many users it covers`,`Accurate data classification`], a:3, why:`DLP rules look for data by type and label. If sensitive data is misclassified, the tool can't recognize it.`, task:`5A5`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A DLP system has run in monitor only mode for years, and nobody reviews its alerts. What is the MAIN concern?`, opts:[`Leaks go unstopped and unseen`,`Its license may cost too much`,`It may slow down email`,`Its rules may be too strict`], a:0, why:`Monitor mode doesn't block, and unread alerts mean nobody responds. The control neither prevents nor detects in any useful sense.`, task:`5A5`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A company stores customer data with a cloud provider. Who remains accountable for protecting it?`, opts:[`The cloud provider`,`The company itself`,`The provider's auditor`,`The customers in the data`], a:1, why:`Responsibilities are shared, but accountability for the data stays with the organization that owns it.`, task:`5A8`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A cloud provider's SOC 2 Type 2 report lists complementary user entity controls. What are they?`, opts:[`Controls the provider tested twice`,`Controls the customer must run`,`Controls the auditor waived`,`Controls the regulator requires`], a:1, why:`They're controls the provider's design assumes the customer operates. Without them, the provider's controls may not achieve their objectives.`, task:`5A8`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`Which control BEST protects company data on an employee's personal phone if it's lost?`, opts:[`A longer password for the account`,`A written rule about losing phones`,`A managed work container it can wipe`,`Turning off the phone's camera app`], a:2, why:`Containerization under device management keeps company data in an encrypted space that can be locked or wiped remotely without touching personal data.`, task:`5A9`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A key IT vendor may be owned by one of the company's directors. Who should approve the contract?`, opts:[`The director concerned`,`The vendor's own counsel`,`The vendor's largest customer`,`Directors with no interest in it`], a:3, why:`Related party transactions should be disclosed and approved by people with no interest in them. Review of chapter 4.`, task:`2B2`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`After full testing, the evidence neither confirms nor rules out a significant matter. How should the auditor report it?`, opts:[`Leave it out until it's resolved`,`Report it as established`,`Not established, with what remains`,`Give the most likely explanation`], a:2, why:`Report what the evidence supports, say the matter isn't established, and name the evidence that could resolve it. Omitting or overstating it misleads. Review of chapter 3.`, task:`1B5`, domain:`Auditing Process`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const P4=((all&&all[4]&&all[4].picks)||{}),asked=P4[7]===1,toldgiles=P4[7]===0,blocked=P4[6]===3,alone=!!G.wenttunnelalone,heard=((all&&all[1]&&all[1].picks)||{})[7]===2;
    return [
    `At twenty to two Teresa forwarded the papers on who owned Pellridge. ${asked?`He'd asked for them through counsel on the sixteenth, in a list that started with Meridian's related party disclosures.`:`He hadn't asked for them. Priyanka had, through Teresa, on the nineteenth, in a two line email that began Why hasn't anyone.`}`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Pellridge Data Services: related party papers (via counsel, extracts)">Meridian related party disclosures, 2019 to 2026: Pellridge not listed.<br>Directors' conflict of interest declarations, S. Kade, 2019 to 2026: "None," each year.<br>Vendor onboarding, Pellridge, June 2019. Owners: "privately held." Beneficial owners: blank. Due diligence: "known to management." Approved: C. Wynn.<br>Letter, counsel for Pellridge: ownership is "confidential and not relevant to the services provided." Share register: not provided.<br>Letter, counsel for the holding company in the state filings: the company "holds no shares in Pellridge Data Services, Inc." Letterhead: the Seventeenth Street suite.</prop>`,
    `He'd wanted the box to say yes since the Tuesday he'd looked up the registered agent for free. It would have joined everything up: the vendor eight weeks old, the foreword about a team Kade knew well, the key on one man's card. So he read them in the order they came, and didn't skip to the letters.`,
    `So he wrote down what the papers showed. Meridian never recorded who owned Pellridge and never checked, and that's a due diligence finding on its own. Kade declared no interest, every year. A holding company tied to a Kade family trust says it holds no shares, which is a statement about itself and nobody else, on the letterhead of the suite that registered both. Pellridge won't produce its share register. <b>Related party: not established.</b> Under it he listed the records that could settle it, and who held each one.`,
    ...(toldgiles?[`He'd told Giles on the sixteenth that it was plain. He wrote to Giles first, before the finding went anywhere, and said it wasn't.`]:[]),
    `What he couldn't write was why any of it had been arranged this way, if it had been arranged at all. The papers didn't say.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Lou" time="2:40 PM">Mercado says yes. Tomorrow 10 AM, property room. Your lawyer, ours, me. You look. You don't touch.</prop>`,
    ...(alone?[`<prop type="text" from="Lou" time="2:41 PM">Mercado read your page. Mine too. One question, about the 2:05. He's fine.</prop>`]:[]),
    `Ten o'clock was after the school run. He checked it against the week before he answered, which a month ago he wouldn't have thought to do, and wrote back <b>10. Nothing.</b>`,
    (blocked
      ?`At five to three he put his coat on. In London, Giles's diary said he wasn't to be rung after three, Denver time, all week. Nobody rang.`
      :`At five to three he put his coat on. If London rang after three, London could have the voicemail.`),
    `Maisie came out at three thirty two, found the car, and got in talking about a chemistry test she thought she'd got wrong in an interesting way. At the flat she stood in the kitchen and looked at the wall, where her mother had told her there'd be boxes.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"You unpacked."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Yesterday."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Took you a year."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Eleven months."</span>`,
    `She found the tea on the shelf, read the box, and looked at him. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"That's the diner one."</span> She made a cup, pulled Dot's face at it, and drank all of it doing her homework at the kitchen table, on the second chair, which still had its price on, while he burned the first two slices of toast.`,
    `She was asleep by ten, in his bed, with the door open a hand's width because she'd asked for it that way. He sat at the table and wrote Tuesday on the back of an envelope, since the whiteboard was across town.`,
    `<b>7:15 Maisie, school. 10:00 property room, W/P 17. 1:00 Elmcroft. 3:30 Maisie.</b>`,
    (heard
      ?`The file note's copy was in his jacket on the back of the chair. Teresa had it, and Priyanka, and an archive in London. He was the only one of them with a guess about the initials, and he didn't put that on the list either.`
      :`The file note was in the file on Wazee Street, logged, its initials written as initials. He didn't put it on the list.`),
    `He propped the envelope against the toaster, where it would be the first thing either of them saw, and set the alarm for six.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch10.mp3`, len:`about 45 minutes`},
  next:`The Red Folder`
});
